@node Automount_feature_review "Automount feature review"
@{b}@{u}Automount feature review@{uu}@{ub}

This document catalogues the expected behaviour of the automount
feature, scenario by scenario, as verified on real hardware. Each
scenario is written as steps plus the expected outcome, so it doubles as
a test case: if the machine behaves differently, that is a defect worth
reporting. The expectations themselves are not immutable - one can
change when it turns out to hide a defect, a logical contradiction, or a
technical limitation - but any such change lands here and in the release
notes, never silently.

Observation tools: @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text} lists @{fg shine}partition.resource@{fg text} (flag pictures like
@{fg shine}P--M-@{fg text} are explained in its guide), serial lines like @{fg shine}[PT] ...@{fg text} come from
a full build at 9600 baud, and @{fg shine}FLAGS 8@{fg text} in @{fg shine}ENV:cfd.prefs@{fg text} adds the
driver's @{fg shine}[MW]@{fg text} / @{fg shine}[MA]@{fg text} lines. The configuration keys are documented in
@{fg shine}automount.guide@{fg text} .

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Main}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Cards_mentioned_in_this_document}

@endnode

@node Cards_mentioned_in_this_document "Cards mentioned in this document"
@{b}Cards mentioned in this document@{ub}

  * @{b}RDB-PFS@{ub} : an RDB-partitioned card with at least one mountable partition named
    @{fg shine}DH0@{fg text} (PFS3, FFS or similar).
  * @{b}RDB-PFS-2@{ub} : a second RDB card whose partition is also named @{fg shine}DH0@{fg text} , but with a
    different start block and size.
  * @{b}GPT-FAT@{ub}: a GPT card with two or more FAT partitions.
  * @{b}MBR-FAT@{ub}: an MBR card with one or more FAT partitions.

Any FAT-formatted card works where a scenario says "MBR-FAT or GPT-FAT";
the specific layouts only matter where a scenario calls for them
(several partitions, a name clash).

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Automount_feature_review}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link 1__Basic_automount}

@endnode

@node 1__Basic_automount "1. Basic automount"
@{b}1. Basic automount@{ub}

  1. Default @{fg shine}cfd.prefs@{fg text} ( @{fg shine}AUTOMOUNT 1@{fg text} , @{fg shine}CONTROL_FAT -d-D+q@{fg text} ; add @{fg shine}FLAGS 8@{fg text} for the
     serial trace).
  2. Insert RDB-PFS.
  3. Pull it and insert MBR-FAT or GPT-FAT.
  4. Read and write a file on each mounted volume.
  5. Pull the card and reinsert it.

Expected: every insert scans and publishes all partitions to
@{fg shine}partition.resource@{fg text} , even with @{fg shine}AUTOMOUNT 0@{fg text} ( @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text} lists them either
way). RDB partitions mount under their on-disk names (e.g. @{fg shine}DH0@{fg text} ), FAT
partitions as @{fg shine}CF0@{fg text} , @{fg shine}CF1@{fg text} , ... The volumes are read/write. On removal the
mounts are torn down or kept per the @{fg shine}UNMOUNT@{fg text} policy; reinserting mounts
the card again.

@{fg highlight}
  Name         Device        Unit Part Src Pri DosType    Text Flags  MFlg Ctrl
  ------------ ------------- ---- ---- --- --- ---------- ---- ----- ----- ----------
  CF0          compactflash.    0    0 GPT   0 0x464154FF FAT. P--M-     0 -d-D+q
  CF1          compactflash.    0    1 GPT   0 0x464154FF FAT. P--M-     0 -d-D+q
@{fg text}

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Cards_mentioned_in_this_document}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link 2__Scheme_swap_in_one_slot}

@endnode

@node 2__Scheme_swap_in_one_slot "2. Scheme swap in one slot"
@{b}2. Scheme swap in one slot@{ub}

@{fg shine}UNMOUNT NONE@{fg text} in the prefs.

  1. Insert RDB-PFS, let @{fg shine}DH0@{fg text} mount.
  2. Pull it.
  3. Insert MBR-FAT or GPT-FAT in the same slot.
  4. @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text}.

Expected: the old @{fg shine}DH0@{fg text} row goes invalid ( @{fg shine}I--M-@{fg text} , its handler still owns
the mount); the FAT partition appears as a NEW row ( @{fg shine}CF0@{fg text} ). A mounted
entry is never rewritten across partition schemes. Under the default
@{fg shine}UNMOUNT@{fg text} the old mount is torn down on the next removal; under
@{fg shine}UNMOUNT NONE@{fg text} it stays until its own card returns.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link 1__Basic_automount}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link 3__Two_cards_with_the_same_RDB_name}

@endnode

@node 3__Two_cards_with_the_same_RDB_name "3. Two cards with the same RDB name"
@{b}3. Two cards with the same RDB name@{ub}

@{fg shine}UNMOUNT NONE@{fg text} in the prefs.

  1. Insert RDB-PFS (@{fg shine}DH0@{fg text} mounts).
  2. Pull it (the row goes @{fg shine}---M-@{fg text}, kept).
  3. Insert RDB-PFS-2 (also @{fg shine}DH0@{fg text}, different start and size).
  4. @{fg shine}dir DH0:@{fg text} and @{fg shine}dir DH0.1:@{fg text}.

Expected: the second mount is uniquified: @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text} shows @{fg shine}DH0>DH0.1@{fg text} ,
@{fg shine}Assign@{fg text} lists both names, and @{fg shine}DH0.1:@{fg text} reads the second card. Two DOS
devices with one name must never appear.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link 2__Scheme_swap_in_one_slot}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link 4__Reuse_of_a_same_named_hand_mountlist}

@endnode

@node 4__Reuse_of_a_same_named_hand_mountlist "4. Reuse of a same-named hand mountlist"
@{b}4. Reuse of a same-named hand mountlist@{ub}

  1. No card in the slot. Mount a fat95 auto-detect DOSDriver named @{fg shine}CF0@{fg text} by hand (
     @{fg shine}compactflash.device@{fg text} unit 0, @{fg shine}LowCyl = 0@{fg text} ).
  2. Insert MBR-FAT or GPT-FAT.

Expected: the automount finds the existing node and reuses it instead of
creating a duplicate: the serial shows @{fg shine}[PT] reusing handler CF0@{fg text} , one
handler serves the partition, @{fg shine}Assign@{fg text} lists @{fg shine}CF0@{fg text} once, and no @{fg shine}CF0.1@{fg text}
appears. Only auto-detect nodes ( @{fg shine}LowCyl = 0@{fg text} ) are reused; a
fixed-geometry node belongs to its own partition and is left alone.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link 3__Two_cards_with_the_same_RDB_name}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link 5__Name_dedup_and_the_partition_selector}

@endnode

@node 5__Name_dedup_and_the_partition_selector "5. Name dedup and the partition selector"
@{b}5. Name dedup and the partition selector@{ub}

Force a name clash the reuse cannot resolve, so the automount uniquifies
to @{fg shine}CF0.1@{fg text} .

  1. Mount a dummy DOSDriver named @{fg shine}CF0@{fg text} by hand on an unrelated device (a small RAM
     or file handler mountlist), so the name is already taken.
  2. Insert GPT-FAT.
  3. @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text}: the automount node is named @{fg shine}CF0.1@{fg text}.
  4. Read a known file from @{fg shine}CF0.1:@{fg text}.

Expected: @{fg shine}CF0.1@{fg text} serves the FIRST FAT partition - the @{fg shine}.1@{fg text} dedup suffix
never shifts fat95's trailing-digit partition selector. The second
partition mounts as @{fg shine}CF1@{fg text} (no clash), and a suffixed @{fg shine}CF1.N@{fg text} would likewise
serve the second.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link 4__Reuse_of_a_same_named_hand_mountlist}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link 6__No_cfd_prefs_anywhere}

@endnode

@node 6__No_cfd_prefs_anywhere "6. No cfd.prefs anywhere"
@{b}6. No cfd.prefs anywhere@{ub}

  1. Remove @{fg shine}cfd.prefs@{fg text} from @{fg shine}ENV:@{fg text}, @{fg shine}ENVARC:@{fg text} and @{fg shine}SYS:Prefs/Env-Archive@{fg text}.
  2. Insert MBR-FAT or GPT-FAT; watch the serial for the delay before
     @{fg shine}[MW] config: src=0@{fg text} .
  3. Pull the card and insert it again.

Expected: the roughly 2 s source retry is paid on the first insert only;
every later insert goes straight through with just the settle delay,
using the built-in defaults.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link 5__Name_dedup_and_the_partition_selector}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link 7__Configuration_parser_edges}

@endnode

@node 7__Configuration_parser_edges "7. Configuration parser edges"
@{b}7. Configuration parser edges@{ub}

  1. Put this exact @{fg shine}cfd.prefs@{fg text} (unterminated quote, 9-digit hex):

@{fg highlight}
  AUTOMOUNT 1
  FLAGS 8
  CONTROL_FAT "+q
  UNMOUNT NONE
  DOSTYPE_FAT 0x4D5344001
  HANDLER_FAT L:CrossDOSFileSystem
@{fg text}

  2. Insert MBR-FAT or GPT-FAT, then @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text}.

Expected: the quoted value ends at its line, so @{fg shine}Ctrl@{fg text} reads @{fg shine}+q@{fg text} and the
following lines stay keys; the serial shows @{fg shine}dostype=4D534400@{fg text} (hex
parsing stops after 8 digits); the partition mounts through
@{fg shine}L:CrossDOSFileSystem@{fg text} with DosType @{fg shine}0x4D534400 MSD.@{fg text} .

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link 6__No_cfd_prefs_anywhere}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link 8__Static_mounts_and_UNMOUNT_STATIC}

@endnode

@node 8__Static_mounts_and_UNMOUNT_STATIC "8. Static mounts and UNMOUNT_STATIC"
@{b}8. Static mounts and UNMOUNT_STATIC@{ub}

  1. Boot without a card. @{fg shine}Mount CFAUX:@{fg text} (a fat95 auto-detect DOSDriver) and
     @{fg shine}dir CFAUX:@{fg text} so the handler is running.
  2. Insert MBR-FAT or GPT-FAT.
  3. Pull the card.
  4. Put @{fg shine}UNMOUNT_STATIC 0@{fg text} in @{fg shine}ENV:cfd.prefs@{fg text} , reinsert the card (the policy is
     stamped during the mount pass), pull it again.
  5. Reinsert once more.
  6. @{fg shine}Assign CFAUX: DISMOUNT@{fg text}.

Expected: (2) the running handler claims the partition first and the
automount backs off: @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text} shows @{fg shine}CF0>CFAUX@{fg text} with the fifth flag @{fg shine}s@{fg text}
(static, follows @{fg shine}UNMOUNT@{fg text} ), no automount @{fg shine}CF0@{fg text} appears. (3) By default the
static mount is torn down like everything else ( @{fg shine}[PT] unmounted CF0@{fg text} );
the next card is automounted under the synthesized name. (4) With
@{fg shine}UNMOUNT_STATIC 0@{fg text} the row shows @{fg shine}S@{fg text} and the pull keeps the mount:
@{fg shine}[PT] static mount kept@{fg text} , row @{fg shine}---MS@{fg text} , @{fg shine}Assign@{fg text} still lists @{fg shine}CFAUX@{fg text} . (5) The
same handler reattaches. The @{fg shine}s@{fg text} / @{fg shine}S@{fg text} letter always shows what the next
removal will do; a changed key takes effect on the next insert. (6) A
cleanly shut down mount unregisters itself and the partition is free
again (see scenario 11); a handler that disappears without an accepted
@{fg shine}ACTION_DIE@{fg text} leaves the partition claimed until a reboot, a fresh @{fg shine}Mount@{fg text} ,
or the next card removal under a tearing @{fg shine}UNMOUNT@{fg text} policy cleans the
leftover.

@{fg highlight}
  Name         Device        Unit Part Src Pri DosType    Text Flags  MFlg Ctrl
  ------------ ------------- ---- ---- --- --- ---------- ---- ----- ----- ----------
  CF0>CFAUX    compactflash.    0    0 GPT   0 0x464154FF FAT. P--Ms     0 -d-D
@{fg text}

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link 7__Configuration_parser_edges}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link 9__One_partition__one_handler}

@endnode

@node 9__One_partition__one_handler "9. One partition, one handler"
@{b}9. One partition, one handler@{ub}

  1. Insert MBR-FAT or GPT-FAT, let the automount mount @{fg shine}CF0@{fg text}.
  2. @{fg shine}Mount CFAUX:@{fg text} (auto-detect DOSDriver on the same device) and @{fg shine}dir CFAUX:@{fg text}.
  3. Pull the card; @{fg shine}dir CFAUX:@{fg text} again.
  4. Put @{fg shine}MOUNT_USED 1@{fg text} in @{fg shine}ENV:cfd.prefs@{fg text}, reinsert the card, repeat step 2.

Expected: (2) the second mount fails and accesses report
@{fg shine}object is in use@{fg text} (error 202); the serial shows no second @{fg shine}[PT] mounted as@{fg text}
; @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text} keeps the single @{fg shine}CF0@{fg text} row; the Workbench icons do not fight.
(3) With the card out the error returns to a plain no-disk. (4) With
@{fg shine}MOUNT_USED 1@{fg text} the second mount succeeds at your own risk: two handlers
cache one volume, reading mostly works, any write corrupts it, and
@{fg shine}partition.resource@{fg text} keeps the original @{fg shine}CF0@{fg text} registration (no
@{fg shine}[PT] mounted as CFAUX@{fg text} ). Never write in that state.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link 8__Static_mounts_and_UNMOUNT_STATIC}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link 10a__Flush_with_the_automount_set_loaded}

@endnode

@node 10a__Flush_with_the_automount_set_loaded "10a. Flush with the automount set loaded"
@{b}10a. Flush with the automount set loaded@{ub}

Requires the modules loaded from disk, not from ROM. A task lister
(Scout, or a CLI one) shows the @{fg shine}CF.MountAgent@{fg text} task the driver runs per
unit; any first @{fg shine}OpenDevice@{fg text} starts it.

Setup:
@{fg shine}LoadModule LIBS:compactflash.automount LIBS:ptable.library DEVS:compactflash.device L:fat95@{fg text}
.

  1. Boot with the set loaded; confirm the @{fg shine}CF.MountAgent@{fg text} task exists.
  2. Insert a card, let it mount, pull it (default @{fg shine}UNMOUNT@{fg text}, everything torn down).
  3. @{fg shine}Avail FLUSH@{fg text} twice.
  4. Insert a card.

Expected: the flush is a NO-OP for the driver. The automount module
keeps the device open permanently, so it can never expunge:
@{fg shine}CF.MountAgent@{fg text} survives the flush and the later insert mounts normally.
(Removing the automount set is not a runtime operation; it takes a
reboot without the @{fg shine}LoadModule@{fg text} line.)

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link 9__One_partition__one_handler}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link 10b__Driver_expunge_without_the_automount_module}

@endnode

@node 10b__Driver_expunge_without_the_automount_module "10b. Driver expunge without the automount module"
@{b}10b. Driver expunge without the automount module@{ub}

Setup: no @{fg shine}LoadModule@{fg text} ; @{fg shine}compactflash.device@{fg text} in @{fg shine}DEVS:@{fg text} , @{fg shine}ptable.library@{fg text} in
@{fg shine}LIBS:@{fg text} , @{fg shine}L:fat95@{fg text} , a hand @{fg shine}CFAUX@{fg text} DOSDriver.

  1. Insert a FAT card, @{fg shine}Mount CFAUX:@{fg text} , @{fg shine}dir CFAUX:@{fg text} - the device open starts the
     unit and its @{fg shine}CF.MountAgent@{fg text} .
  2. @{fg shine}MOUNT CFAUX: SHUTDOWN@{fg text} , then @{fg shine}ASSIGN CFAUX: DISMOUNT@{fg text} (scenario 11) - the
     handler exits and closes the device.
  3. @{fg shine}Avail FLUSH@{fg text} twice.
  4. @{fg shine}Mount CFAUX:@{fg text} and @{fg shine}dir CFAUX:@{fg text} again.

Expected: with nothing holding the device, the flush expunges the driver
cleanly - the agent is handshaked down before the unit, no @{fg shine}CF.MountAgent@{fg text}
task remains, and nothing crashes. @{fg shine}ptable.library@{fg text} (pulled from @{fg shine}LIBS:@{fg text} by
the auto-detect mount) refuses to expunge while @{fg shine}partition.resource@{fg text}
exists, so the resource never points into freed memory, @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text} keeps
working, and a second copy of the resource can never appear. The
re-mount then loads everything afresh and works.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link 10a__Flush_with_the_automount_set_loaded}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link 11__Clean_hand_unmount__MOUNT_SHUTDOWN___ASSIGN_DISMOUNT_}

@endnode

@node 11__Clean_hand_unmount__MOUNT_SHUTDOWN___ASSIGN_DISMOUNT_ "11. Clean hand unmount (MOUNT SHUTDOWN + ASSIGN DISMOUNT)"
@{b}11. Clean hand unmount (MOUNT SHUTDOWN + ASSIGN DISMOUNT)@{ub}

AmigaOS Mount documents @{fg shine}MOUNT <dev>: SHUTDOWN && ASSIGN <dev>: DISMOUNT@{fg text}
as the clean way to unmount a volume by hand; this scenario verifies it
against a static mount. The behaviour rests on the filesystem handler,
not on the automount: @{fg shine}SHUTDOWN@{fg text} works only as well as the handler's
@{fg shine}ACTION_DIE@{fg text} support (Mount's own help notes that not all handlers can be
shut down). The expectations below describe fat95; a different handler
that refuses or misbehaves here is that handler's defect to report and
fix.

  1. As in scenario 8: @{fg shine}Mount CFAUX:@{fg text} , @{fg shine}dir CFAUX:@{fg text} , insert MBR-FAT or GPT-FAT so
     the hand handler claims the partition ( @{fg shine}CF0>CFAUX@{fg text} ).
  2. Open a window on @{fg shine}CFAUX:@{fg text} (or hold a lock), then @{fg shine}MOUNT CFAUX: SHUTDOWN@{fg text}.
  3. Close the window, @{fg shine}MOUNT CFAUX: SHUTDOWN@{fg text} again.
  4. @{fg shine}dir CFAUX:@{fg text}.
  5. @{fg shine}MOUNT CFAUX: SHUTDOWN && ASSIGN CFAUX: DISMOUNT@{fg text}.
  6. Pull the card and reinsert it.

Expected: (2) the shutdown is refused with @{fg shine}object is in use@{fg text} while
anything holds the volume; the handler keeps serving. (3) The shutdown
is accepted silently and the handler exits a moment later; the device
node stays ( @{fg shine}Assign@{fg text} still lists @{fg shine}CFAUX@{fg text} ), and the handler unregisters its
mount on the way out, so the @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text} row returns to @{fg shine}CF0@{fg text} @{fg shine}P----@{fg text} .
Repeating the command then reports device not mounted (no live handler
to ask). (4) The handler restarts from the kept node and claims the
partition again ( @{fg shine}CF0>CFAUX@{fg text} ). (5) Both commands run (the shutdown
returns success); the name is unlinked and the entry stays
published-only, so the partition is free: a fresh @{fg shine}Mount CFAUX:@{fg text} works,
and (6) after a pull and reinsert the automount mounts it as @{fg shine}CF0@{fg text} . Only
a handler that disappears without an accepted @{fg shine}ACTION_DIE@{fg text} leaves the
entry claimed; the next card removal under a tearing @{fg shine}UNMOUNT@{fg text} policy
cleans such a leftover gracefully.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link 10b__Driver_expunge_without_the_automount_module}  @{"Contents" link Main}

@endnode
